  "name": "Zuni songs (Shiwi ceremonial and social dance songs)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Ceremonial and social song of Zuni Pueblo in western New Mexico, sung by kachina dance groups, medicine societies and social dancers through a yearly cycle that culminates in the Shalako ceremony in early winter, together with social dances such as the Comanche dance. In 1890 Zuni songs were recorded on wax cylinder for the Hemenway Expedition, among the earliest ethnographic field recordings, and further Zuni songs were published from recordings in 1957. A generic membrane-drum card stands in for the large double-headed dance drum and a generic shaker card for the rattles.",
